                    <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">The Bottom Line on Rockwell</h2>
                    <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
                        <p>
                            <strong class="text-white">Rockwell is the right choice if:</strong> You want a specific tool that Rockwell makes and other brands do not. The BladeRunner for small varied cutting in a tight space, the JawHorse for working alone on awkward material, the Sonicrafter for affordable oscillating work with universal blade compatibility. Bought as individual specialty tools, particularly corded ones that sit alongside whatever cordless platform you already own, these earn their place.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong class="text-white">Rockwell is the wrong choice if:</strong> You are building a workshop around a cordless battery system. The platform is too small for the compounding economics that make large ecosystems worth committing to, and there is no guarantee the tools you will want later will exist on it. For that job, buy into a major platform and treat Rockwell as a source of individual additions.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            <strong class="text-white">Before you buy:</strong> Confirm which Rockwell you are looking at, because the vintage machinery is a different company entirely. Check that consumables are universal rather than proprietary. Prefer corded versions of specialty tools, since they sidestep the platform problem completely. And check current warranty and service terms, which have changed over the brand's history.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
                            <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Bottom Line:</strong> <strong class="text-white">Rockwell is a specialty brand wearing a famous name, and it is best bought one tool at a time.</strong> The genuinely original products are worth owning if you need what they do. The conventional ones are outclassed by better-supported competitors. Judge each tool on whether it does something nothing else does, and you will buy well.
