                <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Rockwell Tools?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">The modern Rockwell power tool brand is owned by Positec, the same parent company behind Worx. It is not connected to Rockwell Manufacturing or Rockwell International, the American industrial company that owned Delta and built the vintage woodworking machinery many people associate with the name. The two are entirely separate businesses that happen to share a word.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Rockwell the same as the old Rockwell-Delta machines?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No, and this is the most common confusion around the brand. The cast-iron table saws, band saws, and drill presses sold as Rockwell or Rockwell-Delta were built by a different company decades ago and are traded second-hand today. The modern Rockwell brand makes handheld and benchtop consumer power tools. Buying advice for one does not transfer to the other in either direction.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Rockwell and Worx the same brand?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">They are separate brands with the same parent company, Positec, and they share engineering resources, which is why certain tools show an obvious family resemblance. They are marketed differently, with Worx aimed more at home and garden and Rockwell positioned as more workshop-focused. Their battery systems are not a single shared platform, so do not assume packs interchange.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Sonicrafter still worth buying?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">It is still a capable oscillating multi-tool, and the universal-fit accessory compatibility is a genuine long-term advantage because blades are the real ongoing cost. What has changed is the competition: every major brand now makes a good oscillating tool, many of them cordless within large ecosystems. The Sonicrafter is no longer the obvious value pick it was when the category first opened up, but a corded model at the right price is still a sensible buy.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Rockwell have a good cordless battery platform?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">No, and this is the brand's clearest weakness. The cordless range is small compared with platforms carrying hundreds of tools, which means none of the compounding economics that make a big ecosystem worth committing to. Buy a Rockwell cordless tool because you specifically want that tool, and build your workshop around a larger platform instead.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the BladeRunner actually good for?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Small, varied cutting in a small space. Because it holds a jigsaw blade pointing up through a table, you feed the work into it, which makes curves, scroll work, and awkward small pieces far easier than handling a saw across them. With the right blade it handles wood, thin metal, plastic, and ceramic tile. It is not a table saw, will not rip hardwood, and does not replace one.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Rockwell tools good enough for professional use?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Generally no, and they are not designed to be. These are consumer-duty tools in build quality, warranty terms, and service support, aimed at homeowners and hobbyists rather than daily jobsite use. The exception is the specialty tools: a tradesperson might reasonably own a JawHorse or a BladeRunner for the specific job it does, alongside a professional-grade kit for everything else.</p>
                    </div>
